lesson: 149
title: Sum and difference: two amounts from their total and their gap
level: primary-3
stars: 2
explain: If two amounts add up to a total and one is bigger by a difference, add the difference to the total: now both amounts are as big as the bigger one, so halve.
explain: Two classes have 58 pupils together, and one has 6 more: (58 + 6) ÷ 2 = 32 in the bigger class, 32 - 6 = 26 in the smaller.
explain: Careful with giving: if Tom gives Ben 3 marbles and then they have the same, Tom had 3 + 3 = 6 more than Ben, not 3.
vars: a=name b=name t=thing x=10..300 e=1..40
let: d = 2 * e
let: y = x + d
let: s = x + y
need: s <= 700
answer: y
exercise: Two classes have {s} pupils together. One class has {d} more pupils than the other. How many pupils are in the bigger class?
exercise: {a} and {b} have {s} {t} together. {a} has {d} more {t} than {b}. How many {t} does {a} have?
exercise: The sum of two numbers is {s} and their difference is {d}. What is the bigger number?
exercise: {a} and {b} have {s} {t} in all. If {a} gives {b} {e} {t}, they will both have the same number. How many {t} does {a} have?
exercise: A rope {s} cm long is cut into two pieces. One piece is {d} cm longer than the other. How long is the longer piece?
exercise: Two shelves hold {s} books. When {e} books are moved from the top shelf to the bottom one, both shelves hold the same number. How many books were on the top shelf at first?
test: {s} {t} are shared between {a} and {b} so that {a} gets {d} more than {b}. How many {t} does {a} get?
test: {b} has {d} {t} fewer than {a}, and the two of them have {s} {t} altogether. How many {t} has {a} got?
test: Together {a} and {b} own {s} {t}. After {a} passes {e} of {a.his} {t} to {b}, they own equal numbers. How many {t} did {a} start with?
test: A school orchard has {s} trees, apple trees and pear trees. There are {d} fewer pear trees than apple trees. How many apple trees are there?
exercise: The total of two numbers is {s}, and one is {d} greater than the other. Find the greater number.
exercise: {a} has {d} more {t} than {b}. Together they have {s} {t}. How many {t} does {a} have?
exercise: Two teams at a school in Texas have {s} players combined. The larger team has {d} more players. How many players are on the larger team?
exercise: {s} {t} are split between two boxes, with {d} more in the first box than in the second. How many {t} are in the first box?
exercise: {a} and {b} have {s} {t} altogether. When {a} hands {e} {t} to {b}, they have the same number. How many {t} did {a} have at first?
exercise: Two farms in Wales keep {s} sheep in all. The first farm has {d} more sheep than the second. How many sheep does the first farm keep?
exercise: A rope was cut into two pieces, one {d} cm longer than the other. The whole rope was {s} cm long. How long is the longer piece?
exercise: Two buses take {s} fans to a game in Sydney. If {e} fans moved from the first bus to the second, the buses would carry equal numbers. How many fans are on the first bus?
test: What is {a}'s number of {t}, given that {a} and {b} hold {s} {t} between them and {a} holds {d} more than {b}?
test: A library in Ontario has {s} books in two rooms. The second room has {d} books less than the first. How many books are in the first room?
test: Two numbers make {s} when added, and their difference is {d}. What is the larger of the two?
test: {a} gives {b} {e} {t}, and now they each have the same number. Before that, the two had {s} {t} combined. How many {t} did {a} have at the start?
test: Two classes in Christchurch raise {s} dollars together, and the first class raises {d} dollars more than the second. How many dollars did the first class raise?
test: A path {s} m long is made of a grass part and a gravel part; the grass part is {d} m longer. How many metres long is the grass part?
exercise: 2 classes, {s} kids all together, one's got {d} more. how many in the big class
exercise: {a} n {b} got {s} {t}, {a} has {d} more than {b}. how many {a} got
exercise: {s+10} {t} — no wait, {s} {t} between {a} and {b}, {a}'s got {d} more. how many for {a}
